description:
In repos/development-vessel/src/resolvers/gap-to-feature.ts, make the gap picker's pending check cheap first, then let it scan deeper, so the compose lane stops fail-opening onto a pending gap.

TWO OPERATIONS in this one file. Both anchors occur EXACTLY ONCE.

OPERATION 1 — reorder the pending predicate so the free check runs before the expensive one.
Anchor, occurring exactly once:
    const v = verifyGapCondition(g);
Replace these two lines, which currently read exactly:
    const v = verifyGapCondition(g);
    if (v === 'pending') return true;
with nothing — that is, delete both lines. Pass an empty new_string; the tool accepts an empty replacement.

Then anchor on this line, which occurs exactly once:
    return landedAwaitingVerification && !hasMeasurablePredicate;
and replace it with exactly:
    if (landedAwaitingVerification && !hasMeasurablePredicate) return true;
    return verifyGapCondition(g) === 'pending';

The resulting callback body must be, in order: the `const m = ...` metadata read, the `landedAwaitingVerification` const, the `hasMeasurablePredicate` const, then the two lines above. Do not change the metadata reads or the two consts themselves.

OPERATION 2 — widen the scan.
Anchor, occurring exactly once:
export const PENDING_SCAN_MAX = 20;
Replace with exactly:
export const PENDING_SCAN_MAX = 120;

Change nothing else in the file. Do not touch imports or any other function.

WHY OPERATION 1 IS SEMANTICALLY IDENTICAL. The callback currently returns `(verifyGapCondition(g) === 'pending') || (landedAwaitingVerification && !hasMeasurablePredicate)`. After the reorder it returns `(landedAwaitingVerification && !hasMeasurablePredicate) || (verifyGapCondition(g) === 'pending')`. Logical OR is order-independent for the result, so the verdict is unchanged for every gap. What changes is COST: `verifyGapCondition` spawns `git log` per clone, and after the reorder it is not called at all for any gap the free metadata check already identifies as pending.

WHY OPERATION 2 IS NOW AFFORDABLE, AND WHY IT IS NEEDED. chooseFirstActionable scans `scanMax`, then `scanMax * 2`, and if every candidate in that window is pending it returns `ranked[extendedLimit]` WITHOUT checking it — a blind fail-open onto a probably-pending gap that the post-selection guard then refuses. With scanMax at 20 the window is 40, and the picker has been logging `skipped_pending: 40` repeatedly, meaning all 40 were pending and the lane got the unchecked 41st. Measured: four consecutive 30-minute windows with ZERO composes on an idle box (loadavg 3-8, zero compose-cap refusals), and since then an oscillation of 0, 0, 3, 0, 3 composes per window. Raising scanMax to 120 gives a 240-candidate window against a pool of roughly 330 ranked gaps.

The cost of the wider scan is bounded by operation 1: the gaps currently blocking the lane were checked directly and ALL FOUR are caught by the free metadata branch — route-edit-040f827e and route-edit-0da646c8 carry pending_outcome_verification "unknown", route-edit-015d311c and route-edit-f5165aee carry a real sha — so after the reorder they cost zero subprocesses to skip. The expensive path is only reached for gaps the cheap check does not already resolve, and the scan returns on the FIRST actionable candidate.

Keep the existing comments in place; they explain the bound and the fail-open and remain accurate.

